// Known-equivalent API pairs (VERIFICATION-MODEL.md section 3; ADR 0020; ticket M3-009): a member or type the legacy
// side (the solution before the change) uses and the counterpart the modern side (the solution after it) uses. An entry
// equates the two on any runtime that has both, whatever runtimes the pair is on (ADR 0040). The frontend applies an
// entry while lowering the before side only, and every result lists the ids applied to it in
// properties.equivalencesApplied.
//
// A member entry names both members as exact CallIdentity values and gives the modern argument list in terms of the
// legacy call's source arguments: {"arg": n} (the receiver of an instance or extension call is argument 0, and a
// params array's elements count one by one), optionally with "unwrap": true (without its outermost implicit
// conversion) or "convertTo" (through the implicit boxing or reference conversion to that metadata name), or
// {"const": literal, "type": irType}. A call whose arguments the adapter cannot address all of is left as it is.
// A property or event accessor has no source arguments to adapt, so an entry applies to an accessor call only when its
// adapter passes the receiver, the index arguments and a setter's value through in order and unchanged (ticket P2-070).
// Two member entries may name the same legacy member when their adapters address different numbers of source arguments
// (one per params element count): a call takes the first, in file order, whose adapter addresses it (ticket P2-137).
// Two may also name it for two runtimes that each added another modern member; the entry for the later runtime then
// comes first, so a pair whose modern side has both members takes the one its source binds (ticket P2-142).
// An {"arg": n} item may carry "integer": {"bits": w, "min": a, "max": b} (ticket P2-142): the argument, as the item
// takes it, must be an integer whose type converts implicitly to a signed integer of w bits and whose value the frontend
// knows, at the call, to lie in a..b: a compile-time constant inside it, or an argument of a type all of whose values are
// inside it. It is then passed as that signed integer. The range is checked, never assumed: a call whose argument is not
// known to be inside it is left as it is, so the two members need to agree only inside the range, and the "reason" says
// why they do. A member entry may carry "addedIn", the target framework moniker of the first runtime that has the
// modern member: the entry then applies only to a pair whose runtimes cross it, since on any other pair the modern side
// cannot bind that member where the legacy side binds the legacy one.
// {"rest": n} is every source argument from position n to the last, which must be exactly the elements of the legacy
// call's params array, as the System.ReadOnlySpan<T> the compiler builds from the same elements for the modern member's
// params span parameter: a new T[] of the elements read through the cast map of T[] to the span (ticket P2-143).
// A type entry maps a legacy metadata name to a modern one as a sort name.
//
// Soundness (ADR 0020): whenever both members are invoked on adapted arguments, they return the same value and either
// neither throws or both throw the same exception type. Every "reason" says why that holds, including for null. An
// equivalence that needs any further precondition is not an entry; an "integer" range is none, because the adapter
// addresses only the calls it is shown to hold at. The Web API entries equate action results (status
// code observed, body opaque), not serialized responses: the pipeline's serializer (Newtonsoft, PascalCase, on Web API 2;
// System.Text.Json, camelCase, on ASP.NET Core) is configured outside every procedure and is not checked.
//
// Not an entry: ApiController.StatusCode(HttpStatusCode) -> ControllerBase.StatusCode(int), because the enum-to-int
// conversion is explicit and the adapter has only implicit ones.
